WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.562
Why detections as code?

2
00:00:01.562 --> 00:00:05.037
A rule that lives only in a
portal has three problems.

3
00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:07.175
No one reviews it before it runs.

4
00:00:07.175 --> 00:00:10.113
No one can compare what's
deployed with what was approved.

5
00:00:10.113 --> 00:00:11.838
And there's no clean way back.

6
00:00:11.838 --> 00:00:14.425
A rule kept as a file fixes all three.

7
00:00:14.425 --> 00:00:19.350
It's reviewed, deployed disabled, and
enabled by a reviewed change to the file.

8
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.435
It's tested against a
known-bad event it must find,

9
00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:25.212
and a known-good window
it must stay quiet in.

10
00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:28.962
And a weekly export compares what's
deployed with the repository.

11
00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:33.217
The lifecycle itself, author, test,
alert-only, promote and review,

12
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:38.758
comes from the Security Operations and
Telemetry book's Chapter 11, which also says

13
00:00:38.758 --> 00:00:44.137
a rule that reads only Defender tables is
written as a Defender custom detection.

14
00:00:44.137 --> 00:00:47.263
This module puts those
detections in a repository

15
00:00:47.263 --> 00:00:49.644
and deploys them through the Graph A P I.

16
00:00:49.644 --> 00:00:52.323
Rules that correlate across other sources

17
00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:55.200
stay as Sentinel analytics
rules, in that book.
